---
description: |
  Stamps the OCSF record_integrity profile on fully derived + cast
  investigations records: attestation_list[0] with a SHA-256 fingerprint
  over the canonical serialization of the stored shape, chained per case
  via prev_event (chain_uid = metadata.correlation_uid, the case spine).
  Runs AFTER ocsf_derive + ocsf_cast so the fingerprint covers the
  record exactly as stored — the verification lane (verify.tql) then
  recomputes it from investigations.records read-backs. The caller
  supplies the chain tail for this record in `prev_tail` (uid, type_uid,
  fingerprint of the case's previous record); the investigations node's
  ingest pipeline does so via context_enrich on the in-memory
  investigations-chains context (keyed by correlation_uid), and the
  package tests bake it from fixtures. A null/absent prev_tail is chain
  genesis; on a non-open record it is the documented node-restart case
  (chain state is per-node-lifetime memory) and warns below.
args:
  named:
    - name: authority_uid
      type: string
      default: tenzir-node-investigations
      description: The attesting node identity (OCSF authority_uid; the
        chart renders this node as tenzir-node-investigations — the same
        identity the map stamps as src_endpoint.hostname).
---

// Idempotence guard: a record that already carries an attestation (e.g.
// a read-back replayed through the ingest lane) passes through unstamped.
if attestation_list? == null {
  // ---- restart-gap diagnostic ----------------------------------------------
  // A record with no chain tail that is not a case open (class 2005,
  // activity 1) means the node lost its in-memory chain state — node
  // restart — or an evidence pin landed before its case's first record.
  // The chain restarts HERE with prev_event absent: tamper-evidence is
  // best-effort per node lifetime (the verification lane's per-record
  // fingerprint recompute still catches post-hoc mutation across
  // restarts; only the pre-restart chain linkage is lost). The fork keeps
  // the record flowing on the main branch: the assert drops only the
  // branch copy with a WARNING diagnostic in the node's diagnostics
  // stream. The discard sinks the branch (a fork branch must have one;
  // the assert drops everything that reaches it).
  fork {
    where prev_tail? == null and not (class_uid? == 2005 and activity_id? == 1)
    assert false, message="record_integrity chain restart: no chain tail for this record (node lost in-memory chain state); chain continues from here with prev_event absent"
    discard
  }

  // ---- the attestation, pre-fingerprint ------------------------------------
  // OCSF rule (vendored tenzir-ocsf/v1.9.0): objects/attestation.md —
  // "The attestation is computed over a canonical serialization of the
  // event: the entire event, including the attestation's own
  // authority_uid, chain_uid, and prev_event attributes, and excluding
  // only the attestation's fingerprint and signatures." So: assemble the
  // attestation WITHOUT the fingerprint, hash that serialization, attach
  // the fingerprint last. prev_event rides absent on genesis per
  // objects/prev_event.md ("Absent on the first, or genesis, event of a
  // chain") — a null field would serialize differently from a missing one
  // and break read-back recomputation. chain_uid = the case spine per the
  // record_integrity profile: objects/attestation.md — "Stable for the
  // lifetime of the chain. For example, every attestation produced
  // during a single agent session shares one chain_uid."
  if prev_tail? != null {
    attestation_list = [{
      authority_uid: $authority_uid,
      chain_uid: metadata.correlation_uid,
      prev_event: prev_tail,
    }]
  } else {
    attestation_list = [{
      authority_uid: $authority_uid,
      chain_uid: metadata.correlation_uid,
    }]
  }
  // prev_tail is pipeline plumbing, not stored content: the verifier
  // recomputes over the stored shape, so it must leave before the hash
  // input is captured (prev_event above already carries the copy).
  drop prev_tail?

  // ---- the canonical serialization (the crux) -------------------------------
  // print_json alone is NOT canonical: it preserves field insertion
  // order, and the read-back lane cannot reproduce it (ClickHouse sorts
  // the keys of JSON values; reference/operators/from_clickhouse.md —
  // "fields from the catch-all keep neither their original order nor
  // their position relative to other columns"). The sort() function is
  // the canonicalizer: it recursively sorts record fields by name,
  // including records inside lists (reference/functions/sort.md), so
  // print_json(this.sort()) is a pure function of the record's content —
  // proven by the package determinism test, not assumed. The round trip
  // is byte-stable: print_json quotes temporal/ip values as strings
  // (strict JSON), and read_json / the catch-all re-inference restore
  // them to the same types, which print_json serializes identically.
  //
  // The claim that the record carries this profile rides BEFORE the hash
  // input is captured, so the claim itself is covered by the fingerprint.
  metadata.profiles = append(metadata?.profiles?, "record_integrity")
  _hash_input = print_json(this.sort())
  attestation_list = attestation_list.map(a => ({
    ...a,
    // The fingerprint object (vendored objects/fingerprint.md): the
    // algorithm (3 = SHA-256), the value encoding (1 = Hex), and the
    // serialization scheme (99 = Other, source-defined — NOT JCS: print_json
    // pretty-prints with two-space indents, not the RFC 8785 minimal form;
    // "In the case of Other, it is defined by the event source").
    fingerprint: {
      algorithm_id: 3,
      encoding_id: 1,
      serialization_id: 99,
      serialization: "tenzir-sorted-json",
      value: hash_sha256(_hash_input),
    },
  }))
  drop _hash_input
}
